/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - One diode connects the AC source vs = Vm sin θ to the load, where Vm is the peak supply voltage in volts and θ = ωt is the angle in radians.
   - Vdc is the average (DC) output voltage in V.
   - Vrms is the RMS output voltage in V.
   - FF is the form factor and RF the ripple factor, both plain numbers.
   - With an RL load the inductor keeps current flowing after θ = π, so the diode stays on and the load follows vs into its negative half until the current reaches zero at the extinction angle β (between π and 2π).
   - The transformer utilisation factor TUF = Pdc / (secondary VA rating) is only 0.287 here: the secondary works half the time and carries a DC component that pushes the core towards saturation.
   - A centre-tapped transformer has two equal halves, each giving Vm peak measured from the centre tap.
   - Vm is the peak of each half of the secondary in V.
   - The ripple is at 2f, so 100 Hz on a 50 Hz supply.
   - When D1 conducts, the load's positive end sits at +Vm above the centre tap while the anode of D2 is at the far end of the secondary, −Vm.
*/
