/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Connect a capacitor C across the load.
   - Vr is the peak-to-peak ripple in V, Idc the load current in A, f the supply frequency in Hz, C in F; it assumes C discharges almost linearly for the whole gap.
   - R is the load resistance in Ω.
   - Ripple grows with load current, so a capacitor filter suits light loads.
   - An inductor L in series with the load presents a reactance 2πf_r L to the ripple, where f_r is the ripple frequency (2f for full wave), so doubling L halves the ripple current.
   - The L-C (choke-input) filter puts L in series and C across the load: L blocks the ripple current and C bypasses what is left.
   - ω = 2πf is the supply angular frequency in rad/s, L in H and C in F; valid when the choke current is continuous.
   - The choke current stays continuous only while L is above the critical inductance Lc = R/(3ω).
*/
